he longest duration of an overtaking manoeuvre on an urban street, from having the overtaken vehicle ahead to having it behind", "source": "overtaking-manoeuvre durations in naturalistic driving studies"}, {"name": "p.overtake_same_direction_cos", "unit": "ratio", "value": 0.9, "convention": "cosine of the largest heading difference (about 25 degrees) at which two vehicles count as travelling in the same direction while one moves out to pass the other", "source": "lane-change heading angles in naturalistic driving data"}, {"name": "p.passing_sight_time", "unit": "s", "value": 11.0, "convention": "the MUTCD minimum passing sight distance (Table 3B-1, the warrant for a no-passing zone) divided by the prevailing speed it is tabulated for: about eleven seconds of travel across 25 to 70 mph; the sight distance or the clear distance a pass needs is the subject's speed times this time", "source": "MUTCD 2009 Section 3B.02, Table 3B-1"}, {"name": "p.oncoming_heading_cos", "unit": "ratio", "value": 0.9, "convention": "cosine of the largest angle off exactly opposed headings (about 25 degrees) at which two vehicles count as proceeding in opposite directions", "source": "heading geometry of two-way traffic"}, {"name": "p.conflict_horizon", "unit": "s", "value": 3.0, "convention": "time-to-collision threshold of the traffic-conflict technique: a closest approach later than this is not a present danger of a crash", "source": "traffic-conflict technique; FHWA Surrogate Safety Assessment Model"}, {"name": "p.conflict_centre_clearance", "unit": "m", "value": 3.0, "convention": "centre-to-centre distance at the closest approach below which the bodies of a passenger car and another road user are taken to collide or nearly collide: about a car's half-length plus half a body width", "source": "AASHTO passenger-car design-vehicle dimensions"}], "gaps": [], "gap_resolutions": []}
